Things this project would like to be better. Not issues -- issues are blueprints
for building something. These are wishes, and some of them may never become
anything.

--

I would like a player to work out that they cannot see around the corner without
anybody telling them, and to find that interesting rather than broken. The
difference between those two reactions is entirely in how the edge of the light
is drawn.

I would like the tavern's commander to be a real seat at the table and not a
novelty. Somebody whose whole game is moving crockery and lighting fires and
having the barman look up at the right moment. There is no reason that should be
a lesser way to play, and every reason to expect it will feel like one for a
while.

I would like the ruleset to stay genuinely ignorable. Every system like this
eventually grows one game it secretly prefers -- a field in the core that only one
ruleset needs, a default that assumes hit points. The day the server knows what a
hit point is, it is not a system-agnostic tabletop any more, and it will not
announce that day.

I would like the terminal renderer in phase 9 to still be useful, rather than
being a thing that was built to prove a point and then abandoned. A proof that
nobody runs stops being a proof about a year later.

I would like sound. Hearing something you cannot see is the best thing a tabletop
does, and it is the same geometry as light with different constants. It is in no
phase and no document, and it is the largest thing this project is pretending not
to want.

I would like nobody to ever hand-edit a map. Not because the rule is sacred, but
because I have watched the failure: one map gets edited by hand in a hurry, then
the generator that made it is not quite trusted, then everything is hand-edited
and the generator rots. It takes about two months and it is very hard to reverse.

I would like the first person who joins a session to spend zero seconds
configuring their router. This wish is currently losing to the port range, and it
has not lost yet.
